## How To Import Data From Your CRM Or Another External Source Into Conversation Memory


Nobody likes spending too much time on the phone. But it's inevitable that you'll have to call someone about a business transaction, whether it's scheduling a doctor's appointment, placing online orders, getting service for a vehicle, or asking about tech support… and often, you'll have to repeat your account details every single time you make that call. Wouldn't it be nice if the business you were calling already had that information on file and ready to go?


This guide will help the owners of CRM and phone tree systems do just that. In this guide, you’ll learn how to sync customer data from your CRM into[Twilio Conversation Memory](https://www.twilio.com/docs/voice/conversation-memory) .


With that external CRM data ready on demand, your voice agents, chat bots, and IVRs can pick up context immediately on incoming interactions, without making extra database calls while the customer waits.


## Prerequisites


Before starting this tutorial, ensure you have:


- A Twilio Account with a voice/messaging-capable number.
- [.NET 9 SDK](https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download) or later
- Twilio API Key & Secret with access to Conversation products.


## Configuring Conversation Memory


To use Conversation Memory data, you will first need to configure a[Conversation Memory Store](https://www.twilio.com/docs/api/memory/v1/store) in the Twilio console. Conversation Memory is only available in the new Twilio console, so navigate to[OneConsole](https://1console.twilio.com/) and log into your account.


From here, go to **Data > Conversation Memory > Memory Stores** and create a new Memory Store if you do not already have one.


The console gives you a setup checklist to get you started, but for now, you don't have to do anything else to set up. Just create a new Memory Store with the button on the console. To create your solution, you need your memory store ID, which is at the top left of the memory store screen. There should be a convenient button to copy-paste that ID. Keep that ID for the next step.


Memory Stores use machine learning, and you may have to agree to a warning before proceeding. Keep in mind that Conversation Memory is not intended for use with sensitive information.


### Uploading a CSV


If your customer data is downloaded in CSV format, the next step can be done directly in the console. Simply follow the checklist to import your data, until you reach the **Upload CSV** option.


Some[terms](https://www.twilio.com/docs/conversations/memory/key-terms) to understand in Twilio's dashboard, all of which can be tracked by Conversation Memory:


- **Profiles:** The central entity tied to an end user (identified via phone number or customer ID)
- **Traits:** Static or semi-static properties (e.g., VIPStatus: true, PreferredLanguage: "es", AccountTier: "Enterprise").
- **Observations:** Key past events or recent CRM activities (e.g., "Order #9401 delayed in transit", "Opened ticket #1029 regarding billing").


In order for your CSV to be processed, there must be a line for either the customer phone number or email, which will be used as the unique identifier for each customer.


Once your file is uploaded, you can use AI to automatically map some values into Twilio's customer profiles, or, you can create traits and manually select how to map the data from your CSV into those traits. Traits can be in different data types: a string, integer, boolean, or an array of these types, though traits cannot contain nested arrays.


Once your traits are successfully mapped, click **Complete** , and Conversation Memory will import your CSV.


## An automated solution for customer data import


Uploading a CSV via the dash is one solution. However, there is also an automated way to upload your data using API calls, if you'd rather do all your synchronization programmatically. The rest of this tutorial will discuss a solution using .NET 9 or higher. You can use these same APIs to make these calls in your programming language of choice.


### Step 1: Set up and configure your environment


To follow along, open up your IDE of choice. Create a new C# Web API project. In Visual Studio Code, you can do this via the menus, or you can use the CLI:


Copy code


```text
dotnet new web -n TwilioCrmSync
cd TwilioCrmSync
```


Next you will need to install the DotNetEnv dependency so that your environment variables can be securely stored.


Copy code


```text
dotnet add package DotNetEnv
```


Create an *.env* file in your project folder. This will store important secure environmental variables for your project. Use the following text, replacing the placeholder values here with your real values. You can find all of this on your Twilio console. For your API key, you can create a new Standard API key on your Twilio dashboard. Be sure to copy down both the API key, and the API secret, when you create your key, as the API Secret will not be shown again.


Copy code


```text
TWILIO_API_KEY = API_KEY_HERE
TWILIO_API_SECRET = API_SECRET_HERE
TWILIO_MEMORY_STORE_ID = MEMORY_STORE_ID
```


If you are using git, be sure to include a *.gitignore* that ignores the *.env* file so that you are not storing any API keys in git.


If you are syncing with a real CRM, you may also need to include any secrets associated with that CRM store in this file. Adjust depending on your needs. For the purposes of this tutorial, you will be using mocked up data so no further API key is required. Proceed to creating the mocked up data.


### Step 2: Mock up (or prepare) CRM data


