### Step 3: Map CRM entities to Twilio Conversation Memory constructs


Your data will be extracted in whatever way your CRM formats that data. In order to map your data to Twilio's Conversations, you will need to create code that maps the CRM formatted data to Twilio's memory constructs.


Create a *Models* folder in your project. In this folder, create a file named *CrmModels.cs* *.* Paste the following code:


Copy code


```text
namespace TwilioCrmSync.Models;
public record CustomerProfile(
string Phone,
string FirstName,
string LastName,
string Email,
string City,
string State,
string Country
);
```


This code will take in the information from the JSON file and serialize it into strings that Conversation Memory will then process.


Note that this code is using information structure that is already built in to Conversation Memory. If you want to add custom Traits, you can also do this using new Traits and Trait Groups in the console or using the[Trait Groups API](https://www.twilio.com/docs/api/memory/v1/trait-group/create-trait-group) .


### Step 4: Implement Profile lookup and creation


The next step in this tutorial is to make API calls to Conversations to post the CRM data.


Turn your attention to the[Profiles API.](https://www.twilio.com/docs/api/memory/v1/profile/create-profile) You will be creating a profile using this call programmatically.


Create a *Services* folder, and in that folder, a file named *CrmSyncService.cs.* Paste in the following code:


Copy code


```text
using System.Net;
using System.Net.Http.Headers;
using System.Net.Http.Json;
using System.Text;
using System.Text.Json;
using TwilioCrmSync.Models;
namespace TwilioCrmSync.Services;


public sealed class CrmSyncService
{
private const string MemoryBaseUrl = "https://memory.twilio.com";
private readonly HttpClient _http;
private readonly string _storeId;
private readonly string _crmBaseUrl;
private readonly ILogger<CrmSyncService> _log;
public CrmSyncService(HttpClient http, IConfiguration config, ILogger<CrmSyncService> log)
{
_http = http;
_log = log;
var apiKey = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("TWILIO_API_KEY")
?? throw new InvalidOperationException("TWILIO_API_KEY is not set.");
var apiSecret = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("TWILIO_API_SECRET")
?? throw new InvalidOperationException("TWILIO_API_SECRET is not set.");
_storeId = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("TWILIO_MEMORY_STORE_ID")
?? throw new InvalidOperationException("TWILIO_MEMORY_STORE_ID is not set.");
_crmBaseUrl = config["CrmBaseUrl"] ?? "http://localhost:5000";
var basicAuth = Convert.ToBase64String(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes($"{apiKey}:{apiSecret}"));
_http.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new AuthenticationHeaderValue("Basic", basicAuth);
}
public async Task<bool> SyncCustomerByPhoneAsync(string phone, CancellationToken ct = default)
{
var encodedPhone = Uri.EscapeDataString(phone);
var customer = await _http.GetFromJsonAsync<CustomerProfile>(
$"{_crmBaseUrl}/crm/customers/{encodedPhone}", ct);
if (customer is null)
{
_log.LogWarning("No CRM record found for {Phone}", phone);
return false;
}
var profileId = await EnsureProfileAsync(customer, ct);
if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(profileId))
return false;
return await UpdateProfileTraitsAsync(profileId, customer, ct);
}
private async Task<string?> EnsureProfileAsync(CustomerProfile customer, CancellationToken ct)
{
var lookupBody = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { idType = "phone", value = customer.Phone });
using var lookupReq = new HttpRequestMessage(
HttpMethod.Post,
$"{MemoryBaseUrl}/v1/Stores/{_storeId}/Profiles/Lookup")
{
Content = new StringContent(lookupBody, Encoding.UTF8, "application/json")
};
using var lookupResp = await _http.SendAsync(lookupReq, ct);
if (lookupResp.IsSuccessStatusCode)
{
var json = await lookupResp.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(ct);
using var doc = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
if (doc.RootElement.TryGetProperty("profileId", out var pid))
return pid.GetString();
}
var createBody = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new
{
traits = new
{
Contact = new { firstName = customer.FirstName, lastName = customer.LastName, phone = customer.Phone }
}
});
using var createReq = new HttpRequestMessage(
HttpMethod.Post,
$"{MemoryBaseUrl}/v1/Stores/{_storeId}/Profiles")
{
Content = new StringContent(createBody, Encoding.UTF8, "application/json")
};
using var createResp = await _http.SendAsync(createReq, ct);
if (!createResp.IsSuccessStatusCode)
{
var err = await createResp.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(ct);
_log.LogError("Failed to create profile ({Status}): {Error}", createResp.StatusCode, err);
return null;
}
var createJson = await createResp.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(ct);
using var createDoc = JsonDocument.Parse(createJson);
return createDoc.RootElement.TryGetProperty("id", out var newId) ? newId.GetString() : null;
}
private async Task<bool> UpdateProfileTraitsAsync(string profileId, CustomerProfile customer, CancellationToken ct)
{
var payload = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new
{
traits = new
{
Contact = new
{
firstName = customer.FirstName,
lastName = customer.LastName,
phone = customer.Phone,
email = customer.Email,
city = customer.City,
state = customer.State,
country = customer.Country
}
}
});
using var req = new HttpRequestMessage(
HttpMethod.Patch,
$"{MemoryBaseUrl}/v1/Stores/{_storeId}/Profiles/{profileId}")
{
Content = new StringContent(payload, Encoding.UTF8, "application/json")
};
using var resp = await _http.SendAsync(req, ct);
if (!resp.IsSuccessStatusCode)
{
var err = await resp.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(ct);
_log.LogError("Trait update failed ({Status}): {Error}", resp.StatusCode, err);
return false;
}
_log.LogInformation("Synced {FirstName} {LastName} ({Phone}) -> Profile {ProfileId}",
customer.FirstName, customer.LastName, customer.Phone, profileId);
return true;
}
}
```


This code queries a mock CRM endpoint and gets the JSON data for the customers. If a profile exists that matches the phone number for this customer, it looks up that phone number first to avoid creating duplicate entries. Then it updates that profile with the information from the CRM data. If there is no profile for a customer, such as on your first run of the code, it will make a call to` /v1/Stores/{_storeId}/Profiles` and create a new profile for that customer.


### Step 5: Sync external data to Conversation Memory


To finish the demonstration, you will create a sync service that writes CRM updates to the user's Conversation Memory Store.


To wire your synchronized data into Conversation Memory, you will update Program.cs to load crm-customers.json into memory. Then you expose the mock` GET /crm/customers/{phone}` endpoint for each customer, and add a` POST /sync/{phone}` endpoint to trigger synchronization.


Replace the code in your *Program.cs* with the following:


Copy code


```text
using System.Text.Json;
using DotNetEnv;
using TwilioCrmSync.Models;
using TwilioCrmSync.Services;


Env.TraversePath().Load();
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<CrmSyncService>();
var app = builder.Build();
// Load mock CRM data from local JSON file
var jsonOptions = new JsonSerializerOptions { PropertyNameCaseInsensitive = true };
var crmJson = File.ReadAllText("crm-customers.json");
var mockCustomers = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<List<CustomerProfile>>(crmJson, jsonOptions)
?? new List<CustomerProfile>();
// Mock CRM REST endpoint
app.MapGet("/crm/customers/{phone}", (string phone) =>
{
var decodedPhone = Uri.UnescapeDataString(phone);
var customer = mockCustomers.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Phone == decodedPhone);
return customer is not null ? Results.Ok(customer) : Results.NotFound();
});
// Trigger CRM-to-Twilio sync for a specific phone number
app.MapPost("/sync/{phone}", async (string phone, CrmSyncService syncService) =>
{
var decodedPhone = Uri.UnescapeDataString(phone);
var success = await syncService.SyncCustomerByPhoneAsync(decodedPhone);
return success
? Results.Ok(new { status = "synced", phone = decodedPhone })
: Results.NotFound(new { status = "failed", phone = decodedPhone });
}).DisableAntiforgery();
app.Run();
```


## Testing your CRM data


Now you can test your endpoint.


Run your code:


Copy code


```text
dotnet run
```


Now, you can trigger a sync for any of the four mock customers in crm-customers.json using` curl` :


Copy code


```text
curl -X POST http://localhost:5000/sync/%2B15551234567
```


%2B is the URL-encoded + sign in E.164 phone numbers. This, combined with the phone number, is your customer's unique identifier and is how the API decides which customer profile to create.


In URL paths, the` +` symbol must be percent-encoded as` %2B` so the server interprets it as part of the E.164 phone number (` +15551234567` ) rather than a space character.


In your console, you should see` {"status":"synced","phone":"+15551234567"}` if the call went through correctly. Check your Twilio console to see how the data has been imported. If all goes well, you should see customer data imported into your memory store.


## Conclusion


Now you know how to sync up data from your CRM to Twilio's Conversation Memory. What's next? Conversations and Conversation Memory give you a wide variety of options to make customer calls a smooth experience. Check out the documentation, or read more:


